WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.060
Which data would hurt most if it
leaked tomorrow, and who owns it?

00:00:04.295 --> 00:00:05.785
Four ways we lose track.

00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:09.487
First, nobody can name the crown jewels.

00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:13.877
The canteen menu and the M and A
data room get the same permissions.

00:00:14.422 --> 00:00:18.132
So the budget is spread thin,
and the crown jewels get the average.

00:00:18.527 --> 00:00:20.357
A short list of five datasets,

00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:24.157
each with an owner,
changes every decision after it.

00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:27.733
Second, forty copies of the customer file.

00:00:27.988 --> 00:00:33.238
It sits in the CRM, in exports, in two
hundred mailboxes, and in every backup.

00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:36.603
Test systems are often
loaded with real data.

00:00:36.893 --> 00:00:40.543
Protecting the CRM protects
one copy out of forty.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.800
Third, shared with anyone with the link.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.880
Sharing is one click,
and nobody revisits it.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.500
Years later, one phished account opens
every folder it could already see.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.990
Consider internal by default,
and links that expire.

00:00:56.117 --> 00:00:58.987
Fourth, kept for fifteen years,
needed for two.

00:00:59.282 --> 00:01:02.492
Deleting feels risky,
so nothing is ever deleted.

00:01:02.842 --> 00:01:04.752
When the breach comes, all of it leaks.

00:01:05.142 --> 00:01:09.512
Under GDPR, keeping data too
long is itself a violation.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:11.960
What good looks like.

00:01:12.245 --> 00:01:15.815
Three or four labels,
and a named owner for each crown jewel.

00:01:16.145 --> 00:01:18.035
Internal-only sharing by default.

00:01:18.405 --> 00:01:20.215
No production data in test.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.320
A retention schedule
that actually deletes.

00:01:23.455 --> 00:01:26.385
GDPR Article 5 limits storage,

00:01:26.955 --> 00:01:29.985
and Article 30 asks for
a record of what we hold.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.040
A suggested first step.

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:34.555
Ask for one page.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:39.110
The five datasets that would hurt most,
who owns each, where each lives,

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:40.840
and when it is deleted.

00:01:41.215 --> 00:01:44.165
If that page does not exist,
that is the finding.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.240
Four ways we lose track.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.710
You cannot protect what you cannot name.

